  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe approach leverages quadratic programming (QP) with iterative constraint refinement to minimize the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. By formulating the problem as a QP with a focus on the most significant lags, it dynamically adjusts h to reduce the worst-case overlaps. The method balances computational efficiency with optimization effectiveness by prioritizing critical constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Variables: Function values h \u2208 [0, 1]^n and maximum overlap t \u2208 \u211d.\n- Objective: Minimize t.\n- Constraints:\n  - For each lag k, \u2211_{i=0}^{n_points - k - 1} h[i](1 - h[i + k]) * dx \u2264 t.\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1.\n  - \u2211 h[i] = n_points / 2.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na) Initialization:\n- Discretize h into n_points samples (de_fault: 100).\n- Use initial_h_values if provided; otherwise, initialize h uniformly.\n\nb) QP Setup:\n- Define decision variables h and t.\n- Express each correlation constraint as a linear inequality involving h and t.\n\nc) Iterative Solving:\n- Solve QP with active constraints, focusing on lags contributing most to t.\n- Update h and t based on solver output.\n- After each iteration, clip h to [0, 1] and normalize.\n\nd) Termination:\n- Stop when time budget is exhausted or when sufficient improvement is achieved.\n\n4. Search Choices\n- Start with n_points = 100.\n- Use CVXPY with MOSEK for QP solving.\n- Prioritize lags with highest correlation in each iteration.\n\n5. Compute Budget\nEach QP solve is estimated at 10 iterations per second. With 600s, expect 6000 solves, sufficient for convergence.\n\n6. Validation\nEnsure h sum to 50 and each value is within [0, 1]. Validate C\u2085 using np.correlate.",
